'<h2>Stairs Are the Hardest Job a Carpet Ever Gets</h2>',

"<p>A bedroom carpet spreads its traffic across a whole room, and most of the room never gets walked on at all. A staircase gets every trip &mdash; every school morning, every laundry basket, "
"every dog launch &mdash; landed on the same few inches of every tread, in the same spot, thousands of times a year. And unlike flat floor, stair carpet is bent over a hard 90-degree nose edge "
"its entire life, which means the pile at the nose is stretched open and taking impact at the same time.</p>",

"<p>That is why the same carpet that looks fine in a bedroom after fifteen years can look exhausted on the stairs in five. It is not a defect and it is not bad cleaning &mdash; it is physics, "
"and it is exactly why we tell people to shop for the staircase first and the bedrooms second. In Everett this matters more than most places: the split-levels around Silver Lake and the "
"two-story homes off Broadway all run their whole daily life up and down one staircase. It is the main artery of the house, and it deserves the best fiber in the budget.</p>",

'<h2>Density Beats Softness &mdash; Do the Bend Test</h2>',

"<p>Showrooms sell softness, because softness is what your palm notices in the aisle. Stairs do not care what your palm thinks. What survives on a staircase is <strong>density</strong> &mdash; "
"how much fiber is packed into each square inch &mdash; because dense pile supports itself and springs back, while soft, tall, sparse pile crushes flat at the nose and stays flat.</p>",

"<p>You do not need a spec sheet to check this. Take the sample and bend it backwards over your knuckles, the way it will bend over a stair nose. If the pile yawns open and you can see the "
"backing grinning through, that carpet will show its backing on your stair noses within a few years. If the pile stays tight and you see only fiber, it is a stair candidate. We do this test "
"with clients on their own staircase during the estimate &mdash; it takes ten seconds per sample and it eliminates half the board immediately, including some expensive ones.</p>",

'<h2>Fiber: Nylon Earns Its Premium Here</h2>',

"<p>On flat bedroom floor there is a real argument for saving money with polyester. On stairs there mostly is not. Nylon's defining trait is resilience &mdash; it springs back after being "
"crushed &mdash; and the stair nose is the most crushed spot in the house. This is the one place in the home where we tell almost everyone to pay the fiber premium, even if the bedrooms "
"upstairs get something softer and cheaper. Mixed-fiber jobs like that are normal for us, and they put the money exactly where the wear is.</p>",

table('Carpet Fibers, Ranked for Stairs', ['Fiber', 'Stair verdict', 'The honest reason'], [
 ('<strong>Nylon</strong>', 'The stair benchmark', 'Best resilience of any common fiber &mdash; springs back at the nose where everything else crushes flat'),
 ('<strong>Solution-dyed nylon</strong>', 'Best in pet homes', 'Same resilience, plus color that survives the aggressive cleaning stairs in a dog household eventually need'),
 ('<strong>Triexta</strong>', 'Strong second', 'Very good durability and built-in stain resistance; a fine stair answer when the same carpet runs through the whole house'),
 ('<strong>Polyester (PET)</strong>', 'Bedrooms, not stairs', 'Soft and affordable, but it mats under concentrated traffic &mdash; the traffic lane look arrives early at the nose'),
 ('<strong>Wool</strong>', 'Beautiful, situational', 'Naturally resilient and lovely in a low-traffic formal stair; priced accordingly and not for the busiest staircase in a kid household'),
 ('<strong>Berber / looped</strong>', 'Avoid', 'Loops snag on the nose edge and under claws, and a pulled loop on a stair unravels in the most visible spot in the house'),
]),

"<p>If you want the longer fiber conversation, our <a href=\"/blog/best-carpet-for-pets\">pet carpet guide</a> covers solution-dyed fiber in depth and our "
"<a href=\"/blog/the-10-best-carpet-brands-reviews-2023-guide\">carpet brand guide</a> covers who actually builds the dense stair-grade lines. And for neighbors a few miles south, the "
"advice in our <a href=\"/blog/whats-the-best-carpeting-for-stairs-in-kenmore-wa\">Kenmore stair carpeting post</a> holds for Everett word for word &mdash; the staircases are the same, "
"only the commute is different.</p>",

'<h2>Waterfall vs Wrapped: The Install Detail That Shows</h2>',

"<p>There are two ways to carpet a tread, and you can spot the difference from across the room. <strong>Waterfall</strong> lets the carpet flow over the nose and drop straight down to the "
"next tread in one continuous piece &mdash; faster to install, slightly softer look, and the standard on most modern staircases. <strong>Wrapped</strong> (you will also hear cap-and-band or "
"Hollywood style) tucks the carpet tight under the nose so the profile of each step shows crisply &mdash; more tailored, more labor per tread, and worth it on a staircase that is a visual "
"centerpiece.</p>",

"<p>Neither is wrong. What is wrong is carpet that is not fastened firmly at the nose &mdash; that is where loose installation shows up first as a soft, shifting edge underfoot, and a "
"shifting edge on a staircase is a safety problem, not a cosmetic one. It is also why stairs are priced per tread: each one is cut, stretched, wrapped and fastened individually. A quote "
"that buries a staircase inside a square-foot total is worth a follow-up question before you sign it.</p>",

'<h2>Safety: the Part We Bring Up Even When You Do Not</h2>',

"<p>Stairs are where household falls happen, and carpet choice moves the odds. Short, dense pile over a <strong>firm, thinner pad</strong> gives your foot a stable, predictable surface. "
"Deep plush over a soft, thick pad feels luxurious in the hallway and mushy at the stair nose &mdash; the edge compresses underfoot and your footing moves. Of the three pad grades we carry, "
"the right stair answer is usually firmer and thinner than what we would put in the bedroom the stairs lead to, and we will say so at the estimate even though it is the cheaper option.</p>",

"<p>Carpet also simply out-grips hard flooring on stairs. If your stairs are bare wood and you love them that way, a runner gets you traction while the wood still shows &mdash; our "
"<a href=\"/blog/our-stair-installation-services\">stair services page</a> covers the options, and if the treads themselves are worn, "
"<a href=\"/blog/stair-refinishing-cost-per-step\">stair refinishing is priced per step</a> for the same reason stair carpet is priced per tread: each one is its own small job.</p>",

